## The rule

> The count moves only when one entire row becomes proved.

No decrement because a nicer compiler was found. No decrement because two
hypotheses share notation. No decrement because an old source almost proves
something. No decrement because a narrower conditional record was created. A
claim of "3 remain" is invalid unless it is accompanied by a proof of A, B, C or
D, or by a genuine theorem showing one of them is logically implied by another.

The Agda ledger enforces the proof-carrying half of this rule: a row's status
can only be `provedRow` if a `CompletionCertificate` is supplied, and a
certificate must carry a proposition **together with an inhabitant of it**. What
the type system cannot check is that the proposition chosen is the row's
normative statement. The normative statements are the displayed conditions
below; a certificate must quote the one it claims to discharge.

